---
// src/pages/humanoid-on-a-macbook.astro
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = "Training a humanoid on a MacBook";
const publishDate = "November 13, 2026";

const stats = [
  { value: '79 min', label: 'to track all 40 clips' },
  { value: '1,024', label: 'simulated robots' },
  { value: '2.6 GB', label: 'of memory' },
  { value: '0', label: 'NVIDIA GPUs' },
];

const results = [
  { it: '1,000', min: 24, ret: '0.624', failed: 14 },
  { it: '2,000', min: 52, ret: '0.732', failed: 8 },
  { it: '3,000', min: 79, ret: '0.824', failed: 0 },
  { it: '4,000', min: 102, ret: '0.832', failed: 0 },
  { it: '4,500', min: 112, ret: '0.834', failed: 0 },
];

const mm3Code = `# slow on Metal: a broadcast product summed over an inner axis
def mm3(A, B):
    return (A[..., :, :, None] * B[..., None, :, :]).sum(-2)

# 10-20x faster: three column-row products, fused into one kernel
def mm3(A, B):
    return (A[..., :, 0:1] * B[..., 0:1, :]
          + A[..., :, 1:2] * B[..., 1:2, :]
          + A[..., :, 2:3] * B[..., 2:3, :])`;

const eluCode = `# mlx.nn.elu
mx.where(x > 0, x, mx.exp(x) - 1)        # gradient is NaN for x > 88

# what the trainer uses now
mx.where(x > 0, x, mx.expm1(mx.where(x > 0, 0.0, x)))`;
---
<Layout title={`${title} - Project`} active="projects" description="Porting a world-model humanoid motion tracker from NVIDIA GPUs to Apple's MLX and training it on one M1 Pro MacBook in 79 minutes.">
  <main class="page">
    <article class="wrap post">
      <header class="page-head">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker"><a href="/#projects">Projects</a> / humanoid-on-a-macbook</p>
          <h1 class="page-title">{title}</h1>
          <p class="page-caption">
            I ported a world-model motion tracker from a rack of NVIDIA GPUs to Apple's MLX. One M1 Pro with 16 GB now
            teaches a simulated Unitree G1 to walk, run, dance, fight and get up off the floor in 79 minutes.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="page-meta meta">
          <span>{publishDate}</span>
          <span>6 min read</span>
          <span>Python · MLX · MuJoCo · RL</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="zone lead-zone">
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="fig-frame">
            <video autoplay loop muted playsinline controls poster="/mlx-humanoid-rollouts.jpg" width="1280" height="960">
              <source src="/mlx-humanoid-rollouts.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            One policy, four of the 40 clips it tracks. Grey is the simulated G1 driven by the policy; amber is the
            reference motion it is asked to follow.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="stats">
          {stats.map(s => (
            <div class="card stat">
              <span class="stat-value">{s.value}</span>
              <span class="kicker">{s.label}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">01</p>
          <h2>Why this should not work</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Whole-body control for humanoids is usually a data-center job. The standard recipe is model-free
          reinforcement learning over thousands of GPU-simulated robots, and the
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28480">SuperMimic</a> paper puts a number on it: its baseline trains for
          7 days on 128 GPUs.
        </p>
        <p>
          SuperMimic's own answer is a different algorithm. Learn a differentiable world model of the robot from
          simulator data, then improve the policy by backpropagating through short rollouts imagined inside that
          model. The paper reports beating the 128-GPU baseline in about 3 hours on a single GPU.
          <a href="https://nico-bohlinger.github.io/papers/2026_xmimic_xsoma.pdf">XMimic</a>, by Nico Bohlinger and
          Jan Peters, rebuilt that recipe in JAX and tracks the 40 LAFAN1 clips in under a minute on four H100s.
        </p>
        <p>
          All of it assumes CUDA. The simulator is MuJoCo Warp, the learner leans on bf16 tensor cores, and whole
          training iterations are compiled into single GPU programs. A MacBook has none of that. What it does have is
          eight CPU cores, a GPU that shares memory with them, and MLX.
        </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">02</p>
          <h2>Why it works anyway</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The method is sample-efficient, and that changes where the compute goes. Each training iteration needs only
          about 2,500 new simulated steps, because the policy learns mostly from imagination: 512 rollouts of 32 steps
          through the world model, differentiated end to end. So the physics can be slow and the learning has to be
          fast, which is exactly the shape of a laptop.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>CPU, physics.</strong> MuJoCo's plain C engine steps 1,024 robots in a pool of eight threads, about
            22,000 control steps per second. Every robot keeps its own friction and center-of-mass randomization.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>GPU, learning.</strong> The world model, the policy, the critic, the rewards and the forward
            kinematics all run in MLX. The replay buffer is a numpy array, which costs nothing to hand over because
            host and GPU share memory.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="fig-frame"><img width="2808" height="1853" loading="lazy" src="/mlx-humanoid-figure.webp" alt="1,024 simulated G1 robots standing on a MacBook whose display shows the training curve; a running robot with its next 32 imagined steps; the policy next to its reference; and a strip comparing 128 GPUs for 7 days, 1 GPU for 3 hours and 1 MacBook for 79 minutes" /></div>
          <figcaption>
            The loop: the CPU simulates, the GPU imagines and backpropagates, the new policy goes back to the
            simulator. The curve on the laptop's display is this run's own reward. The first two entries of the bottom
            strip are the SuperMimic paper's numbers for its large benchmark; mine is for 40 clips.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">03</p>
          <h2>Same algorithm, checked to six digits</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A port that "seems to train" is worth little, so before the first real run I wrote a test that feeds the
          same inputs to the original JAX code and to the MLX port and compares the outputs: rotations, kinematics,
          motion features, rewards, networks, optimizers, and finally the gradients themselves.
        </p>
        <p>
          All <strong>351 checks</strong> pass. The gradient of the policy loss through 32 imagined steps agrees with
          JAX to a relative error of <strong>2e-6</strong>, in float32 on both sides.
        </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">04</p>
          <h2>What broke on the way</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">

        <h3>Metal hates small matrices</h3>
        <p>
          The first working version took 2.5 seconds per iteration, and the big matrix multiplies were not the
          problem. The 3x3 rotation products were. Thousands of them run in the kinematics and the reward, and both
          obvious ways to write one are slow on Apple's GPU: a batched matmul of tiny matrices, and a broadcast
          product summed over an inner axis.
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{mm3Code}</code></pre>
        <p>
          Automatic differentiation then puts the slow reductions back into the backward pass, so the products also
          got hand-written gradients, which are products of the same kind. Together with precomputing the reference
          motion's kinematics once, forward kinematics with gradients for 16,000 robot poses dropped to 18 ms and an
          iteration to about 1.3 seconds.
        </p>

        <h3>Two compiler bugs</h3>
        <p>
          <code>mx.compile</code> is what makes those formulations fast, and it failed twice. Splitting the same
          intermediate array twice crashes it with a missing-key error, so the rotation code uses slices instead.
          Then the fused gradient kernels grew past Metal's limit on kernel arguments, which a no-op
          <code>mx.contiguous</code> on each custom gradient fixes by acting as a fusion barrier.
        </p>

        <h3>The NaN at iteration 620</h3>
        <p>
          The first long run looked healthy for 16 minutes. Then every policy update started getting skipped as
          non-finite, and the policy silently stopped improving. The loss was finite. Only its gradient was NaN.
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{eluCode}</code></pre>
        <p>
          MLX's built-in ELU evaluates <code>exp(x)</code> on the branch it does not select. For a pre-activation
          above 88 that overflows in float32, and a zero cotangent times infinity is NaN. One imagined rollout with a
          large activation was enough to poison the critic for good. JAX's ELU guards against this; the port now does
          too.
        </p>

        <h3>16 GB is not much</h3>
        <p>
          MLX keeps freed GPU buffers in a cache whose default limit is nearly all of the machine's memory. The
          trainer grew to 7.5 GB, I started a second process next to it, and the laptop went into swap and stopped
          making progress. Capping the cache at 1 GB and giving each physics thread a copy of the robot with its
          meshes reduced to convex hulls (15 MB instead of 75 MB, same contact dynamics) brought the footprint to
          2.6 GB at the same speed.
        </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">05</p>
          <h2>Results</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The run trained for two hours on the 40 G1-retargeted LAFAN1 clips, about 2.4 hours of motion. Each
          checkpoint is scored by playing every clip from its first frame without noise; a clip fails if the robot
          strays too far from the reference in height or tilt.
        </p>
        <div class="card table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Iteration</th><th>Training (min)</th><th>Normalized return</th><th>Failed clips</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {results.map(r => (
                <tr class={r.failed === 0 && r.min === 79 ? 'hit' : ''}>
                  <td>{r.it}</td><td>{r.min}</td><td>{r.ret}</td><td>{r.failed} / 40</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The target used for the H100 runs is a return of at least 0.82 with no failed clip, held for three
          checkpoints. The MacBook gets there at iteration 3,000, after 79 minutes. The hardest clips are the
          fall-and-get-up sequences: a first run that I stopped at 37 minutes failed all six of them, and the full run
          completes them.
        </p>
        <p>The honest fine print:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>An H100 reaches the same target in about 2.5 minutes. The laptop is roughly 30 times slower, at a quarter of the batch sizes.</li>
          <li>The policy tracks body pose closely (4 to 7 degrees of mean joint error) but its position in the world drifts 0.1 to 0.7 m from the reference over 12 seconds.</li>
          <li>My checkpoints are 250 iterations apart and each clip is evaluated in one deterministic world, a coarser protocol than the published one.</li>
          <li>The SuperMimic numbers I quote are from the paper's abstract and are for a far larger motion benchmark than these 40 clips.</li>
          <li>This is simulation only. Nothing here has touched a real robot.</li>
        </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">06</p>
          <h2>Why I care</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Not because a laptop should replace a cluster. It is because the loop of changing something and seeing a
          humanoid move differently now fits on the machine I already carry around, with no queue and no cloud bill.
          An algorithm that needs a thousand times less compute stops being a data-center experiment and becomes
          something you can iterate on from a couch.
        </p>
        <p>
          I did the port in one long pairing session with Claude Code, which wrote most of the code while I steered,
          and the equivalence test is what let me trust the result.
        </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">07</p>
          <h2>Credits</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose credits">
        <p>
          The method is <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28480">SuperMimic</a> (Liao et al.). The trainer I ported
          is <a href="https://nico-bohlinger.github.io/papers/2026_xmimic_xsoma.pdf">XMimic</a> by Nico Bohlinger and
          Jan Peters, itself a JAX rebuild of <a href="https://github.com/fuyichen0725/blinkmimic">BlinkMimic</a> by
          Fuyi Chen. Physics is <a href="https://mujoco.org">MuJoCo</a>, the learner is
          <a href="https://github.com/ml-explore/mlx">MLX</a>, the robot is the Unitree G1. The motions are LAFAN1 by
          Ubisoft La Forge (CC BY-NC-ND 4.0), so the trained policy is for non-commercial use only. The laptop in the
          figure is a model from the pmndrs examples repository.
        </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

